# Independently authored EXPLAIN-modifier silent-drop
# CORRUPTION-class sentinel slice for the GPL MTR-format
# runner. NOT copied or adapted from MySQL's mysql-test/.
#
# Existing coverage (read carefully — DO NOT duplicate):
#   - `EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON SELECT * FROM ep` (MySQL
#     top-level FORMAT= syntax): pinned in
#     explain_basic_extra.test:81. Different shape —
#     unparenthesized MySQL FORMAT= form. Documented
#     there as "no-op stability".
#   - `EXPLAIN (VERBOSE) SELECT * FROM ep`: pinned in
#     explain_basic_extra.test:85. Different shape —
#     VERBOSE alone (no FORMAT). Documented there as
#     "no-op stability".
#   - `EXPLAIN (FORMAT YAML)`, `(FORMAT XML)`,
#     `(FORMAT JUNK)`, `(ANALYZE, BUFFERS)`,
#     `(BUFFERS)`, `(COSTS OFF)`, `(SETTINGS)`,
#     `FORMAT=TREE`: pinned in
#     explain_format_basic_extra.test (lines 81-111).
#     Documented there as scattered "no-op" notes.
#   - `EXPLAIN ANALYZE INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE` (1105
#     errno): pinned in explain_format_basic_extra.test
#     lines 127-140. Different shape — DML targets.
#
# Genuinely ZERO prior coverage on these shapes (grep
# verified across tests/main/*.test):
#   - `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON)` (PG-parenthesized JSON —
#     the form pgAdmin / Datadog APM / monitoring tools
#     emit programmatically). Prior coverage only has
#     the MySQL top-level `EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON` form
#     and the parenthesized YAML/XML/JUNK variants —
#     NOT (FORMAT JSON) specifically.
#   - `EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT)` (control / accidental-
#     match case proving the silent-drop hits even on
#     the spelling that "happens to" be the actual
#     output format).
#   - `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON, VERBOSE)` (multi-option
#     combo — proves both modifiers dropped in the same
#     parens list).
#   - `EXPLAIN (COSTS off, FORMAT JSON)` (multi-option
#     combo with lowercase `off` boolean — proves the
#     boolean-arg form and FORMAT JSON are both
#     silently dropped together).
#   - `EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT, ANALYZE TRUE)` (explicit
#     boolean-arg ANALYZE form — different shape from
#     the bare `ANALYZE` keyword and the `(ANALYZE,
#     BUFFERS)` shape already pinned).
#
# Pre-existing pattern fingerprints this slice joins:
#   - CORRUPTION-class family: PR #421 (DEFAULT
#     subquery stored as text), PR #432 (TIMESTAMP(n)
#     precision silent-drop). Shared mechanism: the
#     modifier parses successfully into the AST, the
#     IR-conversion pass never reads the field, the
#     statement executes with default behavior — apps
#     receive WRONG CONTENT with no error or warning.
#     This slice extends the family to EXPLAIN's
#     option list.
#   - SILENT-DROP family: PRs #391 (INHERITS), #407
#     (SCHEMA AUTHORIZATION), #408 (WITH ORDINALITY),
#     and the SELECT INTO new-table family. EXPLAIN's
#     FORMAT/ANALYZE/BUFFERS options follow the same
#     mechanism.
#
# This slice pins (EIGHT shapes — all EXPLAIN-option
# silent-drops on `SELECT 1`, plus ONE support pin):
#
#   CORRUPTION-class BUG — EXPLAIN option modifiers
#   silently dropped (headline):
#
#     Shape A — `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) SELECT 1`:
#       returns the standard text plan tree, NOT a
#       JSON document. pgAdmin / Datadog APM /
#       monitoring tools that call JSON.parse() on
#       the result will fail with a confusing
#       "unexpected token" SyntaxError far from the
#       actual cause (the silent format-drop here).
#
#     Shape B — `EXPLAIN (FORMAT XML) SELECT 1`:
#       returns the standard text plan tree, NOT an
#       XML document.
#
#     Shape C — `EXPLAIN (FORMAT YAML) SELECT 1`:
#       returns the standard text plan tree, NOT a
#       YAML document.
#
#     Shape D — `EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT) SELECT 1`:
#       returns the standard text plan tree. Control
#       case — matches by accident (TEXT is the
#       always-emitted format), proving the silent-
#       drop is uniform across ALL FORMAT names.
#
#     Shape E — `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON, VERBOSE) SELECT
#     1` (multi-option combo): returns plain text.
#       Both FORMAT JSON and VERBOSE are silently
#       dropped in a single parens list.
#
#     Shape F — `EXPLAIN (COSTS off, FORMAT JSON)
#     SELECT 1`: returns plain text WITH costs still
#       shown. Both the boolean-arg `COSTS off` and
#       `FORMAT JSON` silently dropped.
#
#     Shape G — `EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON SELECT 1` (MySQL
#     top-level `FORMAT=` syntax on a literal SELECT,
#     not on a table): returns plain text. Wire-shape
#       confirmation that the silent-drop spans both
#       the PG parens syntax AND the MySQL FORMAT=
#       syntax — both wire-paths arrive at the same
#       broken behavior. (Cross-references
#       explain_basic_extra.test:81, which pins the
#       same shape on `SELECT * FROM ep`.)
#
#     Shape H — `EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT, ANALYZE TRUE)
#     SELECT 1` (explicit boolean-arg ANALYZE): returns
#       plain text only. NO Execution Time / Actual
#       Rows lines emitted — the query was NOT
#       actually run. ANALYZE silently dropped, just
#       like the `(ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` shape pinned in
#       explain_format_basic_extra.test:91.
#
#     Shape I — `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT 1`:
#       returns plain text only. Same shape as
#       explain_format_basic_extra.test:91 but on a
#       literal-SELECT (DualScan) target. Re-pinned
#       here to make the CORRUPTION-class story
#       self-contained inside this sentinel.
#
#   SUPPORT pin — bare `EXPLAIN SELECT 1`:
#     - Works as expected, returns the standard text
#       plan tree (`Project [1] / DualScan`). Control
#       case: proves the bug is option-modifier-
#       specific, not an EXPLAIN-wide breakage. Apps
#       calling plain EXPLAIN are unaffected.
#
# Big-picture finding:
#
#   This is CORRUPTION-class, not "missing-feature"
#   class. Apps don't get an error or warning — they
#   get a STRING with the wrong content type. pgAdmin,
#   Datadog APM, New Relic, AWS Performance Insights,
#   and every other plan-visualization / APM tool
#   that issues `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) <q>` and then
#   `JSON.parse(result)` will fail at parse time
#   with `SyntaxError: Unexpected token Q` (the `Q`
#   from `QUERY PLAN`). The error is far from the
#   cause — the operator sees a JSON parse error and
#   blames their JSON library, not the database.
#
#   Joins the CORRUPTION-class family established by:
#     - PR #421 DEFAULT subquery (stores subquery as
#       text literal instead of evaluating);
#     - PR #432 TIMESTAMP(n) precision silent-drop
#       (full microseconds returned regardless of
#       declared (n)).
#
#   Same mechanism in all three: the AST node carries
#   the modifier, but the IR-conversion / dispatcher
#   layer never reads the field. No surface telemetry
#   distinguishes "honored" from "silently dropped"
#   at the wire — the result is the same shape it
#   would have been without the modifier.
#
#   ANALYZE silent-drop has a second hazard: in real
#   PG, ANALYZE causes side-effects (the query is
#   actually executed). Apps that assume ANALYZE TRUE
#   inside the parens form will run the query are
#   wrong here — the query is NOT run for the parens
#   form (only the top-level `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` keyword
#   form runs it; see explain_format_basic_extra.test
#   header). Apps using ANALYZE TRUE in parens form
#   to "warm" the planner or trigger a side-effect
#   will silently see no effect.
#
# Wire divergence check: both the MySQL top-level
# `FORMAT=JSON` form and the PG-parenthesized
# `(FORMAT JSON)` form silent-drop identically. The
# silent-drop happens in the EXPLAIN executor (post-
# parse), which is shared by both wires. mysqlwire
# probe (port 13599) confirms identical text-plan
# output for all 9 shapes. pgwire is expected to
# exhibit the same silent-drop (same dispatcher).
#
# Adaptation notes:
#   # zeta-adapt: CORRUPTION-class BUG — EXPLAIN
#     option modifiers (FORMAT JSON/XML/YAML/TEXT,
#     ANALYZE [TRUE|FALSE], BUFFERS, COSTS off,
#     VERBOSE) silently dropped in BOTH the PG-
#     parenthesized form `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) <q>`
#     and the MySQL top-level form `EXPLAIN
#     FORMAT=JSON <q>`. Output is always the standard
#     text plan tree. NO error, NO warning. Tools
#     that JSON.parse() the result will fail with
#     a confusing SyntaxError far from the actual
#     cause. Workaround: do NOT call EXPLAIN (FORMAT
#     JSON) against zeta — issue plain `EXPLAIN <q>`
#     and parse the text-plan tree, or use the
#     top-level `EXPLAIN ANALYZE <q>` form which is
#     functional (see explain_basic_extra.test
#     header) for actual-execution timing.
#   # zeta-adapt: KNOWN DIVERGENCE — `(ANALYZE TRUE)`
#     boolean-arg form is also a silent no-op (no
#     execution, no Execution Time lines). Only the
#     top-level `EXPLAIN ANALYZE <q>` keyword form
#     actually runs the query.

--echo === Shape A: EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) — returns TEXT plan, NOT JSON (CORRUPTION) ===

# Headline BUG. pgAdmin, Datadog APM, monitoring tools
# call this and JSON.parse() the result. They get
# `QUERY PLAN\nProject [1]\n  DualScan` — SyntaxError
# on first token. No error from the database; the
# diagnostic shows up far away in the client.

EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape B: EXPLAIN (FORMAT XML) — returns TEXT plan, NOT XML ===

EXPLAIN (FORMAT XML) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape C: EXPLAIN (FORMAT YAML) — returns TEXT plan, NOT YAML ===

EXPLAIN (FORMAT YAML) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape D: EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT) — control case, matches by accident ===

# Proves the silent-drop is uniform across ALL FORMAT
# names: the dispatcher never reads the FORMAT field,
# always emits the text shape. TEXT just happens to
# match what the dispatcher always emits.

EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape E: EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON, VERBOSE) — multi-option, both dropped ===

EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON, VERBOSE) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape F: EXPLAIN (COSTS off, FORMAT JSON) — boolean-arg + FORMAT both dropped ===

# Costs are still shown (would be hidden if COSTS off
# were honored), FORMAT JSON is dropped. Two silent-
# drops in one parens list.

EXPLAIN (COSTS off, FORMAT JSON) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape G: EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON (MySQL top-level form on literal SELECT) — TEXT ===

# Cross-wire confirmation. The MySQL top-level
# FORMAT= form and the PG parens form converge on the
# same silent-drop. Different parse path, same broken
# executor.

EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape H: EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT, ANALYZE TRUE) — ANALYZE dropped, query NOT run ===

# No "Execution Time:" or "Actual Rows:" lines emitted.
# In real PG, ANALYZE TRUE inside parens runs the
# query (just like the bare ANALYZE form). Here both
# the boolean-arg ANALYZE and the FORMAT TEXT are
# parsed and discarded; the dispatcher emits the
# static plan tree without executing.

EXPLAIN (FORMAT TEXT, ANALYZE TRUE) SELECT 1;

--echo === Shape I: EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) — both dropped on DualScan target ===

# Same shape as explain_format_basic_extra.test:91
# but against the literal-SELECT (DualScan) target.
# Re-pinned to keep the CORRUPTION-class story self-
# contained: ANALYZE silent-drop holds across both
# table and DualScan plan sources.

EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) SELECT 1;

--echo === SUPPORT pin: bare EXPLAIN SELECT 1 — works, control case ===

# Proves the bug is option-modifier-specific, NOT an
# EXPLAIN-wide breakage. Apps calling plain EXPLAIN
# are unaffected; only callers using FORMAT/ANALYZE/
# BUFFERS/VERBOSE/COSTS modifiers in the parens form
# (or FORMAT= top-level form) hit the silent-drop.

EXPLAIN SELECT 1;
